#=GF RT   Variations of the C2H2 zinc finger motif in the yeast genome and
#=GF RT   classification of yeast zinc finger proteins.
#=GF RA   Boehm S, Frishman D, Mewes HW;
#=GF RL   Nucleic Acids Res 1997;25:2464-2469.
#=GF RN   [2]
#=GF RM   12683994
#=GF RT   Assessment by molecular dynamics simulations of the structural
#=GF RT   determinants of DNA-binding specificity for transcription factor
#=GF RT   Sp1.
#=GF RA   Marco E, Garcia-Nieto R, Gago F;
#=GF RL   J Mol Biol 2003;328:9-32.
#=GF DR   INTERPRO; IPR013087;
#=GF DR   PROSITE; PDOC00028;
#=GF DR   PRINTS; PR00048;
#=GF DR   SCOP; 1zaa; fa;
#=GF DR   HOMSTRAD; zf-CCHH;
#=GF DR   SO; 0000417; polypeptide_domain;
#=GF CC   The C2H2 zinc finger is the classical zinc finger domain. The
#=GF CC   two conserved cysteines and histidines co-ordinate a zinc ion.
#=GF CC   The following pattern describes the zinc finger.
#=GF CC   #-X-C-X(1-5)-C-X3-#-X5-#-X2-H-X(3-6)-[H/C] Where X can be any
#=GF CC   amino acid, and numbers in brackets indicate the number of
#=GF CC   residues. The positions marked # are those that are important
#=GF CC   for the stable fold of the zinc finger. The final position can
#=GF CC   be either his or cys. The C2H2 zinc finger is composed of two
#=GF CC   short beta strands followed by an alpha helix. The amino
#=GF CC   terminal part of the helix binds the major groove in DNA binding
#=GF CC   zinc fingers. The accepted consensus binding sequence for Sp1 is
#=GF CC   usually defined by the asymmetric hexanucleotide core GGGCGG but
#=GF CC   this sequence does not include, among others, the GAG (=CTC)
#=GF CC   repeat that constitutes a high-affinity site for Sp1 binding to
#=GF CC   the wt1 promoter [2].
#=GF SQ   151
